        "text": "All this discussion is rather abstract.\nIt can be made much more concrete by moving\nto a convenient matrix representation known as the Kronecker product.\nSuppose $A$ is an $m$ by $n$ matrix, and $B$ is a $p$ by $q$ matrix.\nThen we have the matrix representation:\n\n$$\n A \\otimes B \\equiv \\overbrace{\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc}\n A_{11} B & A_{12} B & \\ldots & A_{1 n} B  \\tag{2.50}\\\\\n A_{21} B & A_{22} B & \\ldots & A_{2 n} B \\\\\n \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\\n A_{m 1} B & A_{m 2} B & \\ldots & A_{m n} B\n \\end{array}\\right]}^{m p \\times n q}\n$$"
      }
